      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright 2000 Harcourt Publishers Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "matrix metalloproteases (MMPs) produced by vascular smooth-muscle cells (VSMCs) degrade extracellular matrix and facilitate the migration of these cells. This is a fundamental process in arterial intimal hyperplasia. This study investigated whether Marimastat (a selective but non-specific MMP inhibitor) can prevent intimal hyperplasia in cultured human internal mammary artery (IMA)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "segments of IMA from 8 patients were prepared and cultured for 14 days in serum-supplemented medium (control) or in medium supplemented with Marimastat at 2 concentrations (treatment groups). The tissue was fixed, sectioned, stained and neointimal thicknesses measured by computer-aided image analysis. Further sections were cultured in the same manner and prepared for gel enzymography to quantify the production of MMPs."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "neointimal thickness was significantly reduced by Marimastat in a dose-dependent manner when compared to controls (p =0.008 Wilcoxon). Gel enzymography demonstrated a reduction in levels of MMP2 and MMP9. This was most significant for the active forms of the enzymes ( p =0.03)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "our results suggest that there is a potential therapeutic role for specific inhibition of the gelatinases in the prevention of human arterial restenosis."
          }
        ]
      },
